panels.description:
Three failure layers in one panel, read top-down:

* `up{job=~"$job"}` — 0 means Prometheus could not reach the target at all: nothing was scraped, and every other panel on this dashboard is showing history;
* `min(llm_monitor_scrape_ok)` — 0 means the monitor is up but *its* scrape of the model server failed (no /metrics route, timeout, 5xx). Different incident, different runbook section, and precisely the distinction the  bespoke 'is it down?' tile could not make;
* `increase(llm_monitor_scrape_success_total[...])` — successful passthrough scrapes in the window, the canonical 'is the monitor actually scraping' signal (ADR 0001 C2);
* `time() - timestamp(up{...})` — how stale the newest sample is, which is how a target that answers but has stopped advancing (a proxy replaying a buffered response) becomes visible.

The `llm_monitor_*` series are deliberately NOT clamped by $job/$instance: 'is the telemetry pipeline itself alive' has to stay answerable while the variables are narrowed to one server, and the `upstream` label already separates several monitors (one proxy can front several servers via `/metrics?url=`).

PromQL sent by this panel:

```promql
A: up{job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
A: min(llm_monitor_scrape_ok)
A: sum(increase(llm_monitor_scrape_success_total[$__rate_interval]))
A: max(time() - timestamp(up{job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}))
```

The payoff of this row: does KV-cache growth turn into GPU memory pressure on this box? Left axis, each series as a fraction of **its own** ceiling:

* **GTT used** — `node_drm_memory_gtt_used_bytes / node_drm_memory_gtt_size_bytes`: the model's side, the pool that weights and KV are actually allocated from, out of the `amdgpu.gttsize` budget;
* **KV cache** — `llamacpp:kv_cache_usage_ratio`, what the *server* says about its KV pool (the same series LLMKVCachePressure alerts read) and the `llm:kv_cache_usage_ratio` alias beside it;
* right axis, bytes: the flat **GTT budget**, so the absolute scale of the claim is on the chart rather than in a footnote.

**Read the shape, not the levels — and if the shape does not correlate, that is the answer.** The denominators are different on purpose (KV is a share of the KV pool; GTT is a share of a ~117 GiB budget that also holds the weights), so a KV cache going empty → full can move GTT by a few percent only. Two curves climbing together means KV growth is what consumes the pool; KV swinging while GTT sits flat means the budget is dominated by something else (a second model, a static context reservation, fragmentation) — in which case this panel has told you something worth knowing and the *Memory pools* panel says what.

Not yet verified over a real traffic window from a development container with no GPU in it: on the box, `monitoring/collect_host_baseline.sh` prints the same two ratios over the last 24 h so the correlation is checked rather than assumed.

Absent is not zero. node_exporter publishes an attribute it could not read as `0` rather than omitting it (the findings note §1.1 explains why: the driver refuses to answer rather than waking a runtime-suspended card), so `0 %` busy or a `0` sensor can mean *could not ask*. A high number is always real; a low one may not be. `python3 spikes/amd-gpu-metrics/amdgpu_sysfs_probe.py probe` prints the same attributes with the honest ok/missing/denied/busy status.

Every `node_*` series on this row comes from the `host` scrape job (node_exporter on the host, `prometheus/targets/host.json`). If this panel is empty while the model panels are fine, the exporter or the scrape path is the problem, not the GPU: `NodeExporterAbsent` / `NodeGpuTelemetryAbsent` say which, and monitoring/README.md has the decision tree. Pinning `$job` to a model job also empties this row on purpose — a host has no model job.

PromQL sent by this panel:

```promql
A: {__name__=~"node_drm_memory_gtt_used_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"} / {__name__=~"node_drm_memory_gtt_size_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
B: host:gtt_usage_ratio{job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance",model=~"$model"}
C: max({__name__=~"llamacpp:kv_cache_usage_ratio|llamacpp:kv_cache_usage",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance",model=~"$model"})
D: llm:kv_cache_usage_ratio{job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance",model=~"$model"}
E: {__name__=~"node_drm_memory_gtt_size_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
```

The four pools behind the ratio above, in bytes, so an absolute claim can be checked without arithmetic:

* **GTT used** (left) against the dashed **GTT budget** — the ceiling is flat by construction (a boot parameter), so any slope is the model;
* **VRAM used** and **visible VRAM used** (left, dashed): the BIOS carve-out. Flat and small is the *correct* shape on this box — see the note at the top of the row, and do not alert on these;
* **MemAvailable** on the right axis, because GTT is carved from it: when GTT climbs, this is what falls. Two axes because 117 GiB of system RAM and a 30 MiB VRAM used on one scale would be a chart with one line on it.

PromQL sent by this panel:

```promql
A: {__name__=~"node_drm_memory_gtt_used_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
B: {__name__=~"node_drm_memory_gtt_size_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
C: {__name__=~"node_drm_memory_vram_used_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
D: {__name__=~"node_drm_memory_vis_vram_used_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
E: {__name__=~"node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
```

Utilisation, thermals and power in one panel, because the interesting questions are causal chains between them (throttling: busy % flat or falling while the temperature sits on the band and power is at its cap).

* **GPU busy %** (left axis, 0-100) and **memory busy %** — the driver's gfx/mem activity. On an APU `mem_busy_percent` counts LPDDR-side activity and is worth reading with care, not as 'HBM is full';
* **temps** on the **right axis in °C**, one line per amdgpu hwmon sensor (the hottest one is what the alerts read);
* **power** has **no axis of its own** — it is drawn on its own hidden scale in watts, so read the legend/tooltip for its value. A third visible axis would be unreadable, and plotting watts against a °C axis would be a lie with better typography.

The findings note warns that on an APU the power sensor reports *package* power, not 'GPU power' — label it honestly in any screenshot.

Absent is not zero. node_exporter publishes an attribute it could not read as `0` rather than omitting it (the findings note §1.1 explains why: the driver refuses to answer rather than waking a runtime-suspended card), so `0 %` busy or a `0` sensor can mean *could not ask*. A high number is always real; a low one may not be. `python3 spikes/amd-gpu-metrics/amdgpu_sysfs_probe.py probe` prints the same attributes with the honest ok/missing/denied/busy status.

PromQL sent by this panel:

```promql
A: max({__name__=~"node_drm_gpu_busy_percent",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"})
B: max({__name__=~"node_drm_mem_busy_percent",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"})
C: node_hwmon_temp_celsius{chip="amdgpu",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
D: node_hwmon_power_average_watt{chip="amdgpu",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
E: node_hwmon_power_cap_watt{chip="amdgpu",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
```

Because the GTT pool is system RAM, the same exhaustion event is visible from the host side — usually *earlier*, because the kernel starts stalling tasks before the driver refuses an allocation:

* **memory PSI** (left axis, fraction of wall clock with ≥1 task stalled on memory) — the expression NodeMemoryPressure{High,Critical} alert on;
* **swap used** on the **right axis in bytes**, against the dashed swap total: on this box swap is the pressure valve after GTT, so a climbing swap line with a flat GTT line is a different problem than both climbing;
* **load1 / load5** have **no axis** (own hidden scale): how many tasks were runnable, which is what 'the box feels slow' actually measures.

Read together with the GPU panels above: GTT pressure + PSI + load rising together is the box running out of memory; GTT alone is a sizing problem in the model config.

PromQL sent by this panel:

```promql
A: max(rate({__name__=~"node_pressure_memory_waiting_seconds_total",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}[$__rate_interval]))
B: sum({__name__=~"node_memory_SwapTotal_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}) - sum({__name__=~"node_memory_SwapFree_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"})
C: sum({__name__=~"node_memory_SwapTotal_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"})
D: {__name__=~"node_load1",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
E: {__name__=~"node_load5",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
```

The row's own instrumentation, so 'the GPU is fine and the telemetry is not' is a panel and not a guess:

* **worst collector** — `min(node_scrape_collector_success)` over the host's collectors: 0 means at least one failed on the last scrape (the `collector` label on the line below says which);
* **drm collector** — the GPU half specifically, the one a partial `/sys` mount breaks;
* **drm scrape duration** on the right axis in seconds: a collector that starts blocking on sysfs is a card that is going away, and it is also the scrape-time budget the `host` job pays;
* **exporter RSS** (no axis, bytes): the footprint claim in monitoring/README.md#footprint, live. It is a budget line, so it is on the dashboard rather than only in a script.

Nothing here is clamped by `$model` (a host has no model), which is deliberate — see the panel note.

PromQL sent by this panel:

```promql
A: min by (job, instance) ({__name__=~"node_scrape_collector_success",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"})
B: {__name__=~"node_scrape_collector_success",collector="drm",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
C: {__name__=~"node_scrape_collector_duration_seconds",collector="drm",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
D: {__name__=~"process_resident_memory_bytes",job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
```
